\section MallocTagOverview Overview

Accounting for memory use in a program is often difficult.  The TfMallocTag
system is designed to track memory use in a hierarchical fashion.  Memory use in
this context refers specifically to heap allocations that you make using
malloc() (and its variants) and free().  Note that this includes the C++ new and
delete operators, since they (in general) simply call through to malloc() and
free() (but see \ref MallocTagBestResults "Best Profiling Results" as well).

The basic idea is that at any point during program execution, you can push a
memory "tag" onto the local call stack.  Allocations made while that tag is
active are "billed" to that tag.  Then later you can query and see how much
outstanding memory is due to that particular tag.  If additional tags are pushed
onto the stack, memory allocations are billed to these "children" tags but are
also included in the bill to the parent tags.

Each line of code in the program that pushes a tag is called a \e call-site.  A
sequence of call-sites is called a \e path node and describes the hierarchy
under which allocations take place.  You use an object (TfAutoMallocTag) whose
constructor pushes the tag and whose destructor pops the tag to control the
lifetime of a tag.  Consider the following example:

\code
#include "pxr/base/tf/mallocTag.h"

void TopFunction()
{
    TfAutoMallocTag tag("Top");	// call-site "Top"

    FuncA();

    malloc(100);		// note1

    FuncB();
}

void FuncA()
{
    TfAutoMallocTag tag("A");	// call-site "A"

    malloc(100);

    FuncB();
}

void FuncB()
{
    TfAutoMallocTag tag("B");	// call-site "B"

    malloc(100);
}
\endcode

Suppose now that you invoke TopFunction().  The program has three different
call-sites, Top, A and B.  However, running TopFunction() generates the
following distinct path nodes: Top, Top/A, Top/A/B, and Top/B.

The total memory billed to path node Top is 400.  Calling TopFunction() results
in allocations in itself, its direct calls FuncA() and FuncB(), and its indirect
call to FuncB() from FuncA().  The direct memory billed to the path node Top is
simply 100.  That is the memory allocation noted by the line in the example
marked \e note1.  Even though this call comes after FuncA() has been called, the
call-site tag A is no longer active, since it was popped off the stack when
FuncA() exited.

Continuing the analysis, the total memory billed to Top/A is 200, the memory
billed to Top/A/B is 100, and the memory billed to Top/B is also 100.  To access
these statistics, you call the GetCallTree() function on the TfMallocTag object.
Note that the system does not begin any actual accounting until Initialize() is
called.  Any memory allocations that occur prior to this point are "off the
radar."

\section MallocTagAddingTags Adding Tags

The following example shows a typical use of tags in a library.  Note that
memory tags in a program should be distinct to avoid unintended aliasing, so
follow the same basic guidelines that apply to avoiding name-conflicts in
functions and classes.

\code
#include "pxr/base/tf/mallocTag.h"

void FancyMesh::Assemble()
{
    TfAutoMallocTag tag("FancyMesh::Assemble");

    // code that does a lot of allocation
    // code that calls CreateVertex(), CreateFace() a lot...
    ...
    ...
}

void FancyMesh::CreateVertex()
{
    TfAutoMallocTag tag("FancyMesh::CreateVertex");
    ...
}

void FancyMesh::CreateFace()
{
    TfAutoMallocTag tag("FancyMesh::CreateFace");
    ...
}
\endcode

\section MallocTagVaryingTags Varying Tags

So far, all the tags shown in examples have been string literals.  Sometimes it
is useful to construct tag names on the fly, as in the following example:

\code
void LoadModel(string const& name)
{
    TfAutoMallocTag tag("LoadModel (" + name + ")");
    ...
}
\endcode

This technique can generate any number of different call-sites (each distinct \p
name passed in generates a different call-site) and thus a whole sequence of
different path nodes.

Unless a dynamic string provides substantial value (as including the model-name
may above), prefer a string literal or a \c TfEternalString (such as the one
returned by \c TF_FUNC_NAME()) where you have the choice.  Either of these is
substantially cheaper, because the implementation can key on the string's
address rather than its characters.  A \c std::string built on the fly does not
get that fast path: it always resolves by content, and additionally costs the
construction of the string itself on every call, whether or not tagging is
active.  Extremely performance-intensive code should pass a string literal or a
\c TfEternalString; failing that, a previously constructed string is still
better than building one on every call.

Be aware also that a call site passing dynamic names can generate a very large
number of distinct call sites and path nodes, each of which is retained for the
life of the process.  A handful of such sites can dominate the total path node
count.  If you find yourself generating unbounded distinct names, consider
whether a fixed set of tags with the varying part omitted would answer your
question.

\section MallocTagPushPop Manual Push / Pop

Occasionally, using local variables to delimit the scope of a tag isn't
possible.  You can make manual calls to Push() and Pop(), but whenever possible
use TfAutoMallocTag.

\section MallocTagReadingResults Reading Results

TfMallocTag::GetTotalBytes(), TfMallocTag::GetMaxTotalBytes() and
TfMallocTag::GetCallTree() all report on allocations that are currently live.

\warning These calls are not cheap.  Each one brings the accounting fully up to
date first, which requires briefly stopping every thread that is recording
allocations so their in-flight data can be collected.  Do not poll them in a
loop or call them on a per-frame basis; treat them as you would a profiling
operation.  If you need a running memory figure, sample it at a coarse interval.

Everything these calls return is a snapshot.  On a running multithreaded
program, allocation activity continues while the snapshot is being assembled and
immediately after it is returned, so treat the numbers as accurate to a moment
that has already passed rather than as an instantaneous truth.

TfMallocTag::GetMaxTotalBytes() is a high-water mark sampled at each internal
consolidation, rather than a continuous one.  The background consolidator wakes
on every buffer spill and on a periodic timer (see \ref MallocTagConfiguration),
and is additionally forced by the queries above, so sampling is frequent -- and
it is densest when allocation is most active, since spill rate is what drives
it.  Even so, a spike that rises and falls entirely within one consolidation
interval is not observed.

TfMallocTag::Clear() resets all accounting and counters while leaving the system
running, so subsequent reports cover only allocations made after the call.
TfMallocTag::Shutdown() stops tracking altogether and returns overhead to its
lowest level; call Initialize() again to resume, and note that anything
allocated while shut down is untracked thereafter.

\section MallocTagPausing Pausing Collection

TfMallocTag::PauseControl is a scoped object that suppresses attribution of
allocations, either on the calling thread (PauseThisThread()) or across all
threads (PauseAllThreads()).  Collection resumes when the object is destroyed.
This is useful for excluding a region whose allocations would otherwise obscure
what you are trying to measure.

Note that pausing suppresses the reported \e bytes, not the underlying
bookkeeping cost.  A paused allocation is still tracked internally, because the
eventual free has to be matched against something.  So PauseControl is a tool
for focusing collection on areas of interest, not for making a hot region cheap.

\section MallocTagPerformance Performance Impact

Programs that have tags but have never called Initialize() have no measurable
increase in running time: pushing and popping a tag is an inline read of a
global and a branch.

With tagging active, the system intercepts every malloc() and free() in the
process, so the cost lands on whatever code is allocating.  For typical
workloads, measured overhead has been around 10 percent of total wall-time.  A
program that does nothing but allocate and free could see substantially more.

Where that cost goes, in rough terms:

\li Each malloc() and free() appends a small record to a per-thread buffer.
    Most allocations are short-lived, and a matched allocate/free pair that
    occurs close together is recognized on the allocating thread and discarded
    outright, so the majority of allocations never cost anything beyond that.
    This is the single most important reason the overhead is as low as it is.
\li Records that do survive are handed off in batches to a dedicated background
    thread, which does the work of maintaining the live-allocation accounting.
    That work is deliberately kept off the threads doing the allocating.
\li Pushing a tag normally requires no lock at all.  A tag that is pushed and
    popped without allocating anything under it does no lookup work whatsoever,
    which covers a large fraction of pushes in practice.
\li Popping a tag never takes a lock.
\li Each distinct call-site and path node reached during the program requires a
    small, permanent amount of memory.  There is a one-time cost to create each,
    and it is not repeated.

The system also reserves address space for its internal tables; see \ref
MallocTagConfiguration.

TfMallocTag::GetPerfStats() reports on the tagging system's own internal
behavior.  It is intended for people working on TfMallocTag itself rather than
for users of it.

\anchor tf_MallocTag_BestResults
\section MallocTagBestResults Best Profiling Results

The memory statistics can be fooled by programs which contain their own
allocators: in this case, the memory requested by the allocator itself does not
correlate well with what tags are active.  A pool allocator that grabs a large
block under one tag and then hands pieces of it out under many others will bill
the entire block to the first tag.  It is best to turn off as many internal
pooling allocators as possible when profiling.

TfMallocTag attributes an allocation to whatever tag was active at the moment
malloc() was called, which is exactly what you want when the allocation is made
on behalf of the work you tagged, and misleading when a cache or a pool defers
or batches it.  When a result looks wrong, suspect this first.

\section MallocTagMultithreading Multithreading

The TfMallocTag system is thread-safe.  Each thread has its own tag stack, so
tags pushed on one thread do not affect attribution on another.
TfMallocTag::GetCallTree() and TfMallocTag::GetTotalBytes() may be called at any
time and from any thread, subject to the cost described in \ref
MallocTagReadingResults.

Because each thread has its own stack, work handed to a task scheduler is by
default billed to whatever tags happen to be active on the worker thread that
picks it up, which is usually not what you want.  TfMallocTag::StackState and
TfMallocTag::StackOverride exist to bridge that gap: a call site that spawns
parallel tasks can capture its own tag stack with
TfMallocTag::GetCurrentStackState() and pass it to the tasks, each of which
installs it with a scoped StackOverride so its allocations are billed to the
spawning site's tags.

\section MallocTagConfiguration Configuration

Three environment settings control how much memory TfMallocTag uses internally.
None normally needs changing.

\li \c PXR_TF_MALLOC_TAG_EVENT_BUFFERS_MB (default 16) sizes the pool of
    recycled event-collection buffers, reused to absorb spills without
    malloc/free churn.  This bounds only the recycle pool; each live thread also
    owns a buffer it is currently filling, and spilled buffers awaiting
    consolidation are not counted against this limit, so total event-buffer
    memory under load can exceed it.
\li \c PXR_TF_MALLOC_TAG_CONSOLIDATE_PERIOD_MS (default 23) sets the period in
    milliseconds between opportunistic wakes of the background consolidator.  On
    each periodic wake, the consolidator drains all threads so that
    slowly-allocating or stalled threads contribute to the running total and
    high-water sampling while their data is still current.  A value of 0
    disables periodic waking.
\li \c PXR_TF_MALLOC_TAG_PATH_NODE_TABLE_MB (default 128) sizes the hash table
    mapping path nodes, rounded down to a power-of-two number of buckets.  This
    is address space, not resident memory: only the buckets actually touched are
    committed, so a program that creates few tags pays little of it.  Raise it
    if TfMallocTag warns that the table is overloaded during memory profiling.
    An overloaded table simply performs suboptimally; it does not suffer data
    loss.

*/

